+---
+name: gemm-optimization-journey
+description: fp16 GEMM A100 kernel — what worked, the perf ladder, and the occupancy ceiling analysis
+metadata:
+  type: project
+---
+
+A100 fp16 GEMM (task-1, `matmul_f16/`). Perf ladder (task1 100-round, 4096³, fp32 accumulate, err ~3e-5):
+- v1 WMMA baseline 62 → v2 +cp.async double-buf 74 → v3 mma.sync+ldmatrix+3-stage 140 → v4 64×64 warp (occ collapse) 129 → v6 templated (found 128-reg occupancy cliff) → v7 **register double-buffered frag pipeline 173** → +STAGES=4 **178** → **+cp.async issue moved to first k-substep (more lead time before wait) 195**.
+
+Best config = **128×256 block, BK=32, 2×4 warps (warp 64×64), STAGES=4, mma.m16n8k16, ldmatrix.x4 (A) + ldmatrix.x2.trans (B), fp32 accumulate** = v7 (`matmul_f16_v7_pipe.cu`), ~195 TFLOPS (62% of 312 peak).
+
+Key lessons (verified by ncu, not guessed):
+- **Register count is an occupancy cliff**: 128 regs/thread (256-thr block) = 2 blocks/SM; 129+ = 1 block → instant ~25% perf loss. Use `__launch_bounds__(threads, MIN_BLK)` to cap.
+- **64×64 warp tile wins** despite only 8 warps/SM (12.5% occ, 252 regs, 1 block): bigger tile cuts MIO bytes/flop (=1/WM+1/WN) so ldmatrix stops saturating the MIO pipe. Smaller tiles (64×32=155, 32×64=170) have higher occupancy but lose to MIO. acc=128 regs makes 2 blocks impossible for 64×64.
+- **cp.async/pipeline TIMING matters hugely**: issuing the next tile's cp.async at the FIRST k-substep (vs last) gave 178→195 by giving the async copy a full tile of lead time before its wait → barrier stall 0.62→0.04.
+- x4.trans for B did NOT beat x2.trans (MIO is byte-bound not instruction-bound). Manual ldmatrix/mma interleave was WORSE (loads frags too late). Threadblock rasterization/swizzle was WORSE (natural order already L2-friendly, 91% hit). Precomputed addresses: neutral.
+- Ceiling at 195: tensor pipe 82%, dominant stall = `wait` (mma fixed-latency) which is occupancy-bound; short_scoreboard ~0 (frag latency fully hidden by the reg pipeline). L2 at 71%.
+
+Sweep harness: config is one-constexpr-per-line at bottom of the templated `_v7`/`_v10` file; sed each line, `./task1.sh build`, run binary `-t 100`. (clang-format wraps multi-var constexpr lines → single-line sed fails; keep one per line.)
+
+**CRITICAL — GPU clock is LOCKED at 1155 MHz** (constant even at 99% util / 346W; not the 1410 MHz nominal). So the REAL achievable fp16 peak on this box = 312 × 1155/1410 ≈ **255 TFLOPS**, not the 312 in CLAUDE.md. Thus v7's 195 = **76% of real peak** (not 62% of 312). Do NOT change clocks (env-fixed benchmark condition, no permission). When sampling clock with nvidia-smi, the test spends seconds in CPU cBLAS ground-truth first (GPU idle) — sample mid-run or you'll catch the idle phase.
+
+Dead ends (verified, don't retry): fp16 accumulate (mma f16.f16.f16.f16) — error 0.017-0.021 (borderline >0.02) AND the essential double-buffer reg pipeline keeps it at 1 block/SM anyway, so no speedup. Single-buffer frags to force 2 blocks = 136 TFLOPS (pipeline loss >> occupancy gain). BK=16 = 50 TFLOPS (syncs every 16 K too frequent). BK=64 = 181. Bigger blocks (256×256) spill/break. Stream-K analyzed and ruled out for 4096³: tail is only 5.2% (512 blocks/108 SMs = 4.74 waves, kernel 714µs) and the partial-reduction workspace I/O (~107 split tiles × 256KB + reduction pass ≈ 40µs) ≈ the tail savings → **net ~zero**. Stream-K only wins at few-wave (small) problems. So ~195 is the validated ceiling for this approach; further gains would need SASS-level scheduling (82%→~85% tensor, ~1-2%) — not tractable with nvcc.
